here and with that the  climate yeah and in the mid to late 60s  wow that's so far ahead of its time well  indeed and you were talking about long  periods of time and he was born in 1919  i mean the amount of environmental  change you'd have witnessed in that more  than a century it's kind of staggering  isn't it  and i think that really shaped his his  sensitivity for the earth and for gaia  because he'd grown up um with his father  a naturalist who taught him the birds by  their song the different flowers and the  hedgerows and then he saw you know  industrial agriculture  um kill off those birds and the  butterflies and everything else and he  saw the world the world he knew near him  changing he'd grown up in brixton in  london  um  and i think that must have informed his  sensitivity he had for blimey we are  profoundly  disrupting our life support system and  then he gave us this amazing insight  into that life support system on a  planetary scale  well tim you must be really missing such  a a powerful thinker an influential man  thank you very much indeed for your time  thank you tom  now we've not had much time for many of  your questions this week but here is one  wayne asks in the light of water  shortages and the fires we saw here in  the uk last week whether we could use  seawater to both put out wildfires and  prevent them by soaking the land and the  answer to that is in short yes and no  in an emergency water is scooped up from  the sea by firefighting aircraft like  this one off a beach in spain and dumped  on or near a blaze to hold it back  but dr joel hershey from the national  oceanography center told us that in the  long term seawater can be very damaging  only a few terrestrial plants such as  mangroves like these ones in senegal can  live on sea water the salt content  otherwise actually dehydrates most  plants and causes them to die  and of course we'd like more questions  from you so go to sky news dot com  forward slash climate or any of ou